nd the record is 69\u00b0F (1964). The low of <strong>33\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13.","t_rec":"Albany has nearly stopped setting record lows","p_rec":"If Albany's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Albany has set <strong class=\"sig\">17 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 64 that chance alone would give, and <strong>92 record highs</strong>, more than the 64 that chance alone would give. The 2010s hold the most record highs of any decade (38). Albany's all-time high is 104\u00b0F, on July 4, 1911.","t_chg":"Albany's nights have warmed since 1986. Its days have warmed.","p_chg":"Across 152 complete years: Albany's average low shows no clear change: \u22120.06\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.13 to +0.01) that includes zero. It's close, but the range still includes zero. Albany's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.17\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.11 to +0.22), about +2.5\u00b0F since 1874. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.97\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.65 to +1.29) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.62\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.23 to +1.01). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in none; 7 months fell. Highs rose in 8 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Albany has gotten wetter","p_rain":"Albany's yearly precipitation has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.45 inches per decade</strong> (95% range +0.24 to +0.66), about +6.8 inches since 1874. The 1991\u20132020 normal is 40.6 inches a year. The wettest year was 2011 (53.68 in); the driest, 1964 (21.55 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 3 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}